WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.526
An Orchard Core site that works as an OpenID Connect server uses a

00:00:05.526 --> 00:00:08.984
signing key and an encryption key for its tokens.

00:00:08.984 --> 00:00:13.322
The OpenID Connect Secrets feature keeps those keys as secrets.

00:00:13.322 --> 00:00:17.660
In this video, you enable it, create the keys, and select them.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:27.986
By default, the OpenID Connect server generates its own signing and encryption certificates.

00:00:27.986 --> 00:00:31.667
It saves them as files in the data folder of the site, on

00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:33.420
the server that runs it.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.202
A server that finds no such files generates certificates of its own.

00:00:38.202 --> 00:00:42.233
A site on several servers, or in containers that start with an empty

00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.230
disk, can end up with different keys on each one.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.220
With the OpenID Connect Secrets feature, the keys are secrets:

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:56.269
encrypted, listed on the Secrets page, and kept in a secret store, the database by default.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:01:01.570
Servers that share the database and the Data Protection keys, which decrypt the

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.690
secrets, read the same ones.

00:01:07.975 --> 00:01:11.260
Open Tools, Features, and search for OpenID.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:18.225
The OpenID Connect Secrets feature is listed with the other features of the OpenID Connect category.

00:01:18.681 --> 00:01:25.196
Its description says it allows storing OpenID Connect signing and encryption keys as secrets.

00:01:25.196 --> 00:01:28.881
The badges beside it show its direct dependencies:

00:01:28.881 --> 00:01:32.501
OpenID Connect Authorization Server, and Secrets.

00:01:32.971 --> 00:01:34.126
Select Enable.

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:39.381
A message confirms that the feature was enabled, and its row shows the Enabled badge.

00:01:43.635 --> 00:01:47.911
Open Tools, Security, Secrets, and select Add Secret.

00:01:47.911 --> 00:01:50.810
The dialog lists the secret types.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:58.855
RSA Key stores cryptographic keys for signing, encryption, or verification, so select Create on its card.

00:01:59.345 --> 00:02:00.408
Enter a name.

00:02:00.408 --> 00:02:05.389
The server settings use it to reference the secret, and it cannot be changed later.

00:02:05.389 --> 00:02:07.795
Let's call this one ServerSigningKey.

00:02:08.257 --> 00:02:13.559
Key size sets the size of the generated key pair, so leave it at 2048 bits.

00:02:13.559 --> 00:02:20.299
The description and the expiration date are optional, and the expiration date is only a reminder.

00:02:20.299 --> 00:02:21.477
Select Save.

00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:26.646
Create a second RSA Key secret the same way, for the encryption key.

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:32.147
Name it ServerEncryptionKey, leave the other fields as they are, and select Save.

00:02:32.626 --> 00:02:37.655
The Secrets list shows both secrets with badges for their type, RSA Key,

00:02:37.655 --> 00:02:40.878
their store, Database, and their expiration.

00:02:40.878 --> 00:02:44.746
With no expiration date, each one reads Never expires.

00:02:49.007 --> 00:02:55.537
Open Settings, OpenID Connect, Authorization Server, and scroll to the RSA Keys from Secrets card.

00:02:55.537 --> 00:03:00.559
It says to use RSA keys stored in Secrets instead of managed certificates,

00:03:00.559 --> 00:03:05.017
and that they take precedence over the certificate store options.

00:03:05.513 --> 00:03:11.072
Signing Key Secret lists the RSA Key and X.509 Certificate secrets.

00:03:11.072 --> 00:03:16.478
Its hint asks you to pick one to use for signing JSON Web Tokens.

00:03:16.478 --> 00:03:18.763
Select ServerSigningKey.

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:23.423
Encryption Key Secret works the same way, and its hint asks you to

00:03:23.423 --> 00:03:26.365
pick one to use for encrypting access tokens.

00:03:26.365 --> 00:03:28.269
Select ServerEncryptionKey.

00:03:28.743 --> 00:03:29.761
Select Save.

00:03:29.761 --> 00:03:34.001
The page warns that the website might be restarted upon saving, and a

00:03:34.001 --> 00:03:38.072
message confirms that the OpenID server configuration was updated.

00:03:38.072 --> 00:03:40.503
The card keeps both secrets selected.

00:03:44.703 --> 00:03:50.128
If the server settings already name a signing or an encryption certificate, enabling

00:03:50.128 --> 00:03:55.131
the feature saves each one as an X.509 Certificate secret that references it,

00:03:55.131 --> 00:03:57.663
and selects that secret in the card.

00:03:58.413 --> 00:04:01.673
A list left on None keeps the server on its certificates.

00:04:01.673 --> 00:04:05.399
So does a secret that cannot be loaded, and the server records the

00:04:05.399 --> 00:04:06.693
problem in its log.

00:04:08.693 --> 00:04:11.462
That is the OpenID Connect Secrets feature:

00:04:11.462 --> 00:04:16.604
the signing and encryption keys of the server, kept as secrets in the Secrets module.

00:04:16.604 --> 00:04:18.073
Thanks for watching.

